NAME
    mk - orchestrate a project's development lifecycle

SYNOPSIS
    mk [options] [--] <goal> [<goal> ...]
    mk --watch [options] [--] <goal> [<goal> ...]
    mk [options] --goals
    mk [options] --show-goal <goal>

DESCRIPTION
    mk is the project development-lifecycle orchestrator of the technical m layer.

    A project is described by declarative JSON in mk.json at the project root.
    Without --project, mk searches the current directory and then parent
    directories for the first mk.json.

    Goal names are project data. Names such as build, test, run, clean or docs
    have no built-in semantics unless the project defines them.

    A goal selects one or more root operations. Operations may have prerequisite
    operations and may execute an action. The first action type is process.

    Version 2 additionally supports project-to-project dependencies, declarative
    external requirements, context-derived file collections, trusted operation
    providers, declarative operation guards, named outputs, incremental freshness
    and runtime plan refinement. mk.json remains data; it does not name or embed
    arbitrary JavaScript resolver modules.

    The lifecycle engine is implemented in JavaScript and requires an
    installed/default RumiAI-managed nodejs package. mk does not install that
    runtime implicitly.

OPTIONS
    --project <path>
        Select <path> as the project root instead of performing upward discovery.

    --profile <profile>
        Apply the named profile overlay from mk.json.

    --watch
        Run a version-2 lifecycle request initially and again whenever its
        authoritative trigger identity changes.

        Watch is an execution mode, not a project goal or mk.json namespace.
        It is incompatible with --plan, --goals and --show-goal. The first
        baseline has no public polling interval, debounce, host notification
        backend or stop-on-cycle-failure option.

        Every trigger-resolution pass and lifecycle cycle starts through a fresh
        m bootstrap. A temporary invalid root or active-child configuration pauses
        the session without running lifecycle work; the last valid trigger
        baseline is retained until valid resolution returns. Failed lifecycle
        cycles are reported and the session waits for another relevant trigger
        change. SIGINT/SIGTERM terminate the session and are forwarded to an
        active lifecycle child.

    --plan
        Resolve the requested goal set from declarative configuration and the
        currently observable project/runtime context without executing project
        operations.

        For version 1, write the existing line-oriented fully resolved operation
        plan. For version 2, write structured JSON describing currently relevant
        collections, providers and operations. A collection/provider that cannot
        yet be resolved remains pending; an operation whose guard lacks required
        evidence remains conditional.

    --goals
        List the available goal names in ascending lexical order.

    --show-goal <goal>
        Resolve one goal. Version 1 writes JSON containing goal, roots and the
        fully resolved operation list. Version 2 writes goal, roots and the same
        structured plan used by --plan.

    --
        End option processing. Remaining operands are goal names.

WATCH EXECUTION (VERSION 2)
    Watch trigger identity is derived by trusted mk resolution from the selected
    normalized model and currently reachable lifecycle evidence. It includes
    declared operation inputs, reachable collection member content, process
    executable/effective-environment identity, resolved requirement/provider
    identity, incremental fingerprints and incremental declared-output validity.

    Active project dependencies contribute recursively through child-owned opaque
    trigger digests; their internal lifecycle graphs are not flattened into the
    parent watch identity.

    Ordinary non-incremental outputs that are neither consumed as declared inputs
    nor incremental freshness evidence are not watch triggers merely because their
    bytes change.

    The first implementation uses portable polling only as an internal wakeup
    mechanism. Polling cadence is not public project semantics.

PROJECT CONFIGURATION
    mk.json requires a supported numeric version.

    Version 1 accepts these top-level members:

        version
        environment
        goals
        operations
        profiles

    Version 2 accepts:

        version
        environment
        goals
        operations
        collections
        providers
        dependencies
        requirements
        profiles

    Unknown members are rejected.

    goals maps each goal name to an array of root operation/provider names.

    In version 1, operations accept prerequisites and action.

    In version 2, operations additionally accept:

        requirements
            Optional array of named external requirements. The operation cannot
            become ready until all current requirements are satisfied.

        inputs
            Optional named path/collection/output data inputs. Input identity is
            independent from incremental reuse policy and may be declared by an
            otherwise ordinary non-incremental operation.

        when
            Optional declarative condition controlling whether the operation is
            selected when its operands become known.

        outputs
            Optional map of named outputs. Each output currently declares a
            pathname relative to the project root unless absolute.

        failure
            "stop" (default) or "continue". stop preserves normal fail-fast
            behavior. continue records a failed execution result so later
            conditions can observe it and refinement can continue. A failed
            operation still does not satisfy an ordinary prerequisite.

        incremental
            Optional incremental-freshness declaration for an ordinary process
            operation. The operation must declare at least one named output.

PROCESS ACTION
    The process action has this shape:

        {
          "type": "process",
          "command": "mvn",
          "args": ["package"],
          "cwd": ".",
          "env": {
            "NAME": "value"
          }
        }

    command is required. args, cwd and env are optional. cwd defaults to the
    project root; relative cwd values resolve from the project root. mk does not
    implicitly invoke a shell for process actions.

COLLECTIONS (VERSION 2)
    The first collection type is files:

        {
          "type": "files",
          "root": "src",
          "suffixes": [".c", ".cpp"],
          "include": ["main.cpp"],
          "exclude": ["old.cpp"],
          "after": ["generate"]
        }

    root is required. Discovery is recursive below root.

    Without include, mk discovers the current regular files below root. This is
    the normal declarative form when the directory itself defines the intended
    source set; mk.json does not need to copy the directory inventory.

    include is an explicit relative-path selection used when intended membership
    differs from the default discovery. exclude removes exact relative paths from
    either selection form. suffixes filters selected relative paths by suffix.

    after is an optional array of operations/providers that must complete
    successfully before the collection becomes authoritative during execution.
    Until then the collection remains pending. This prevents a generated-source
    collection from treating an old output directory as the result of the current
    lifecycle request.

    A missing collection root with no pending after barrier resolves to an empty
    collection.

PROVIDERS (VERSION 2)
    providers are a trusted mk boundary that derives ordinary operations from
    declarative input. Project configuration selects a registered provider type;
    it does not provide an executable resolver pathname/module.

    The first provider type is map-process:

        {
          "type": "map-process",
          "collection": "sources",
          "prerequisites": ["prepare"],
          "inputs": {
            "config": {"path": "config/${item}.cfg"}
          },
          "outputs": {
            "artifact": {"path": "out/${item}.o"}
          },
          "incremental": {},
          "action": {
            "type": "process",
            "command": "tool",
            "args": ["${item}"]
          }
        }

    inputs, outputs and incremental are optional. Without them, map-process retains
    its original non-incremental behavior.

    For every resolved collection item, map-process derives one ordinary process
    operation. ${item} is substituted in command, args, cwd and env values of the
    provider action template, in provider path-input values and in declared output
    pathnames. Collection-input references and output-input operation/name
    references remain literal lifecycle references.

    Every derived member receives a trusted private path input named $item whose
    pathname is the concrete collection item. Project configuration cannot define
    that private input name. It makes mapped-item content part of ordinary
    operation identity without duplicating the map relation in mk.json.

    Provider inputs use the same path/collection/output forms as ordinary
    operation inputs. Provider outputs use the same named-path output form.
    Provider incremental opt-in uses only:

        "incremental": {}

    The legacy ordinary-operation incremental.inputs compatibility form is not
    accepted on provider templates. An incremental provider must declare at least
    one named output.

    Each derived incremental member reuses the existing ordinary-operation
    fingerprint, output-verification, freshness-record and up-to-date machinery.
    There is no provider-level aggregate freshness record. Member identity is
    based on provider identity plus collection-item pathname identity rather than
    collection enumeration position, so add/remove/reorder of collection
    membership does not by itself invalidate unchanged reachable members.

    A provider may also declare a requirements array. Its derived work cannot run
    until those named requirements are currently satisfied.

    The provider name acts as an aggregate operation. Operations/goals may depend
    on it. The aggregate is satisfied after the collection is resolved, provider
    requirements and prerequisites are satisfied, and every derived item operation
    plus collection after barriers are satisfied. An up-to-date derived member
    satisfies this relation like any other up-to-date operation.

FACILITY REQUIREMENTS (VERSION 2)
    requirements declares named external conditions. The first type is facility:

        {
          "type": "facility",
          "facility": "java",
          "constraints": [">=21", "<26"]
        }

    facility is a provider-independent pkg facility identity. constraints uses the
    existing pkg facility compatibility language.

    Operations and providers refer to named requirements through their requirements
    arrays. Only requirements reachable from the requested lifecycle are queried.

    mk resolves a facility requirement through:

        pkg requirement resolve <facility> <constraint>...

    That package-owned query uses the configured system facility default and
    validates the selected installed provider against every compatibility
    constraint. mk does not create package-consumer bindings, install packages,
    change provider defaults or choose a provider implicitly.

    A reachable requirement is satisfied or unsatisfied. Unsatisfied requirements
    remain visible in --plan and block only their consumer nodes. Execution
    re-resolves them during normal runtime refinement, so an explicit prerequisite
    may change relevant external state before the consumer becomes ready.

    Facility requirements are gates, not a second command/environment projection
    layer. Facility commands/environment remain supplied by normal m/pkg bootstrap
    semantics.

OPERATION INPUTS AND INCREMENTAL FRESHNESS (VERSION 2)
    Operation inputs are first-class lifecycle data identity:

        {
          "inputs": {
            "source": {"path": "src/main.c"},
            "sources": {"collection": "sources"},
            "generated": {"output": {"operation": "generate", "name": "artifact"}}
          },
          "incremental": {},
          "action": {
            "type": "process",
            "command": "cc",
            "args": ["..."]
          },
          "outputs": {
            "artifact": {"path": "out/app"}
          }
        }

    inputs is a named map. The first supported source forms are path, collection
    and another operation's named output. An output input is a data dependency on
    its producer and does not require the same relation to be repeated as an
    ordinary prerequisite merely for reachability. Collection inputs make their
    collection part of the operation's reachable data relation.

    inputs does not itself enable caching or assert that the action is pure.
    Without incremental, the operation executes normally whenever reached.

    incremental opts an ordinary process operation into freshness reuse using the
    shared operation inputs. The operation must also declare at least one named
    output.

    The previous incremental.inputs form remains accepted for compatibility and is
    normalized to the same shared operation input map. A non-empty inputs map and
    a non-empty incremental.inputs map on the same operation are rejected as an
    ambiguous duplicate declaration.

    Incremental identity is content-based. Supported regular files and directory
    trees are fingerprinted deterministically with SHA-256; modification time is
    not freshness identity. The effective operation definition, process
    environment, resolved executable identity, selected concrete providers for
    referenced facility requirements and resolved named inputs also participate in
    the operation fingerprint.

    A previous success is reusable only when the current effective fingerprint
    matches and every declared output still exists in supported fingerprintable
    form with the same recorded output fingerprint. Unsupported or corrupt
    freshness metadata produces a conservative miss and ordinary execution.

    A verified hit has state up-to-date. It satisfies ordinary prerequisites,
    collection after barriers and named-output evidence in the current request.
    It does not fabricate process-result evidence. If a reachable condition
    observes result.status, result.ok, result.signal or result.error for an
    otherwise up-to-date producer, that producer executes in the current request.

    Only successful executions with complete fingerprintable declared outputs
    refresh freshness metadata. Failed execution never creates reusable freshness
    state.

    Freshness metadata is non-authoritative user cache state resolved through:

        state-path user sys mk cache

    Private layout below that semantic cache area is an mk implementation detail.
    Project identity is based on the canonical project root, so a copied/moved
    checkout starts with a cache miss in this baseline.

    --plan may read existing freshness metadata and report up-to-date, but it does
    not create or refresh persistent cache state.

    This baseline stores freshness metadata only. It does not store or restore
    artifact bytes or provide a shared/remote artifact cache.

    The same per-operation freshness machinery also applies to derived map-process
    members when their provider template opts into incremental: {}. No aggregate
    provider cache is introduced, and stale freshness/output cleanup for members
    that later become unreachable is not implied.

CONDITIONS (VERSION 2)
    An operation when condition is declarative. Supported operators are:

        eq ne lt lte gt gte
        truthy
        not

    eq/ne compare resolved scalar values for equality/inequality. lt/lte/gt/gte
    compare resolved scalar values using the current JavaScript runtime ordering.

    An operand may be a JSON scalar literal or one source object:

        {"result":{"operation":"attempt","field":"status"}}
        {"output":{"operation":"build","name":"artifact","property":"size"}}
        {"state":{"path":"marker","property":"exists"}}

    Result fields are:

        status
        ok
        signal
        error

    Output/state properties are:

        exists
        size

    A result operand becomes known after the referenced operation actually has a
    terminal execution result. An output operand is tied to that current-run
    result: after producer success its declared pathname is observed; after
    producer failure, exists is false while size remains unavailable. An old
    pathname left by an earlier request is therefore not treated as a newly
    produced output. State is observed from the current filesystem when the
    condition is evaluated.

    A condition reference creates an observation dependency: the guarded operation
    waits for the referenced operation's evidence, but that relationship is not an
    ordinary success prerequisite. This allows a failure declared continue to be
    observed by a fallback branch.

PROJECT DEPENDENCIES (VERSION 2)
    dependencies declares project-to-project lifecycle relations. A dependency
    currently has this shape:

        {
          "project": "../core",
          "goals": {
            "build": ["compile"],
            "check": ["verify"]
          },
          "profile": "release"
        }

    project is required. Relative paths resolve from the declaring project root.

    goals maps requested parent-project goals to requested child-project goals.
    An unmapped parent goal does not activate that dependency. If multiple
    requested parent goals map to the same direct dependency, their child goals
    are de-duplicated and sent as one child request.

    profile is optional. The parent profile is not inherited implicitly by the
    child project; a child profile is selected only when the dependency declares
    one explicitly.

    Active dependencies are executed by separate recursive mk engine processes.
    The child owns its own operations, providers, collections, conditions and
    further project dependencies. The parent does not flatten that graph.

    Active direct dependencies must succeed before parent-local lifecycle work
    begins. Recursive project cycles are rejected from canonical project identity
    in the active invocation chain.

    Independent sibling branches are independent lifecycle requests. A diamond
    dependency may therefore request the same downstream project more than once;
    no request-wide exactly-once/cache semantics are implied by dependencies.

PROFILES
    Version 1 profiles may contain environment, goals and operations.

    Version 2 profiles may additionally contain collections, providers,
    dependencies and requirements.

    Profile entries replace same-named base entries and may add new entries;
    absent base entries remain inherited. Environment entries replace
    same-named base values. No profile is selected implicitly.

EXECUTION
    Multiple goal operands form one requested set. They do not imply semantic
    left-to-right ordering between otherwise independent goals.

    Version 1 retains the fully resolved sequential executor.

    Version 2 first delegates active project dependencies for the requested goal
    set and requires them to succeed. It then repeatedly resolves only the dynamic
    definitions, external requirements and declared data dependencies reachable
    from the requested local goals, establishes verified up-to-date work when
    possible, executes ready work, records results and refines the reachable plan.
    Context-known files may therefore be concrete before the first action while
    generated files remain pending until a generator runs.

    Normal prerequisites require success (or a conditionally skipped operation).
    A failed continued operation is terminal evidence but does not satisfy an
    ordinary prerequisite.

    The executor is sequential. This is an implementation property rather than a
    semantic ordering rule for otherwise independent operations.

    Process actions inherit standard input, standard output and standard error.
    Their environment starts from the mk caller environment and is overlaid by
    project, profile and action environment entries in that order.

OUTPUT
    Normal successful execution adds no required mk output beyond action output.

    --goals writes one goal name per line.

    Version-1 --plan writes one operation name per line in planned execution order.

    Version-2 --plan writes JSON with:

        version
        goals
        dependencies
        requirements
        collections
        providers
        operations

    Active dependency entries include the dependency name, canonical child project
    root, requested child goals, optional explicit child profile and nested child
    plan. Planning recursively asks child mk instances for plans but does not
    execute their lifecycle work.

    Reachable requirement entries include requirement name/type, facility,
    constraints, satisfaction state and the selected concrete provider when
    satisfied. Operation/provider entries include named and currently unsatisfied
    requirements.

    Resolved collection entries include current items; pending collections include
    waitingFor. Provider entries identify their collection, current state and
    derived operation names. Operation entries include state, prerequisites and
    normalized inputs, plus observation/condition/derived-provider information when applicable.

    Version-2 operation states are ready, blocked, conditional, skipped,
    up-to-date, completed or failed. Provider states are pending, resolved or
    completed; collection states are pending or resolved.

EXIT STATUS
    0   Success.
    1   Project/configuration/resolution/execution/runtime failure.
    2   Invalid command invocation.

FILES
    <project-root>/mk.json
        Declarative project configuration.

REQUIREMENTS
    nodejs
        An installed/default RumiAI-managed nodejs package provides the current
        JavaScript runtime used by the lifecycle engine.

EXAMPLES
    Execute a project-defined build goal:

        mk build

    Resolve without executing:

        mk --plan build

    Select a profile:

        mk --profile release build

    Watch a version-2 build goal:

        mk --watch build

    Request multiple goals as one plan:

        mk test package

    List goals:

        mk --goals

    Select another project:

        mk --project ../other-project build

